---
import Page, { repo } from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Workflow from '../components/Workflow.astro';

// Keep `/` in the Worker so it can choose between this page and install.sh.
export const prerender = false;

let seealso = [
	['/usage', 'usage', 'the daily loop'],
	['/commands', 'commands', 'every flag'],
	['/memory', 'memory', 'Why: trailers'],
	[repo, 'github.com/lukeed/lane', 'source'],
];

let anchors = [
	['fn verify', 'a declaration, by kind and name'],
	['verify', 'a uniquely named declaration'],
	['#script, #style', 'a top-level block in .svelte, .vue, html'],
	['## Rate limiting', 'a markdown section'],
	['@file', 'the whole file (default)'],
];

let tiers = [
	[' ', 'fresh', 'The anchored span is unchanged.', 'Nothing.', 'ok'],
	['~', 'content-changed', 'The implementation changed.', 'Flagged until you resolve it.', 'drift'],
	['!', 'contract-changed', 'The declaration changed.', 'Flagged until you resolve it.', 'drift'],
	['x', 'anchor-missing', 'The symbol no longer resolves.', 'Moved to .lane/attic/.', 'gone'],
];
---

<Page
	title="lane — copy-on-write worktrees with memory that survives them"
	description="Copy-on-write Git worktrees with memory that survives them."
>
	<main class="shell">
		<section class="sec sec-first" aria-label="Name">
			<p class="sec-head sec-head-mute">Name</p>
			<div class="sec-body">
				<h1 class="name">
					<span class="wordmark">lane</span>{' '}
					<span class="tagline">
						<span class="name-dash" aria-hidden="true">&mdash; </span>Copy-on-write Git worktrees with memory that survives them.
					</span>
				</h1>
				<p class="lede">
					<b>git worktree add</b> gives you a clean checkout and none of the ignored files that make it fast: <b>node_modules</b>, <b>target/</b>, <b>.env</b>. You reinstall and rebuild from scratch, even though those caches are already on disk. Whatever the last branch learned about a file stays in that branch.
				</p>
				<p class="lede">
					<b>lane new</b> clones ignored paths by reference, sharing bytes on disk. The new tree starts quickly with its prewarmed cache(s).
				</p>
				<div class="prompt">
					<code class="prompt-cmd"><span class="p">$ </span>curl -fsSL https://lane.lukeed.com | sh</code>
					<button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="curl -fsSL https://lane.lukeed.com | sh">copy</button>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="sec">
			<h2 class="sec-head">Workflow</h2>
			<div class="sec-body">
				<p class="lede">
					Left is the disk: one set of blocks, every tree pointing at it. Right is what you type.
				</p>
				<Workflow />
				<p class="note">
					If trunk is protected, use <b>lane push</b> instead of <b>lane merge</b>. After the pull request lands, <b>lane prune</b> removes the worktree.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="sec">
			<h2 class="sec-head">Description</h2>
			<div class="sec-body reveal">
				<p class="tagline tagline-solo">A second worktree that costs no disk and no cold build.</p>
				<p class="lede">
					Lane asks git for every ignored path — nested ones like <b>packages/a/node_modules</b>, single files like <b>.env</b> — and clones each by reference. On APFS, btrfs, and XFS with <b>reflink=1</b>, the two copies share the same extents. The second tree takes no new space, and the build cache is already warm.
				</p>

				<table class="tbl tbl-cmp">
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th scope="col">In the new tree</th>
							<th scope="col">git worktree add</th>
							<th scope="col">lane new</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">tracked files</th>
							<td data-label="git worktree add">checked out</td>
							<td data-label="lane new">checked out</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">node_modules, at any depth</th>
							<td class="cell-cost" data-label="git worktree add">absent</td>
							<td class="cell-free" data-label="lane new">exists, by reference</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">target/, dist/, .venv/</th>
							<td class="cell-cost" data-label="git worktree add">absent</td>
							<td class="cell-free" data-label="lane new">exists, by reference</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">.env and the rest of .gitignore</th>
							<td class="cell-cost" data-label="git worktree add">absent</td>
							<td class="cell-free" data-label="lane new">exists, by reference</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">uncommitted work</th>
							<td class="cell-cost" data-label="git worktree add">absent</td>
							<td class="cell-free" data-label="lane new">with --dirty</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">what earlier branches learned</th>
							<td class="cell-cost" data-label="git worktree add">absent</td>
							<td class="cell-free" data-label="lane new">lane why &lt;file&gt;</td>
						</tr>
						<tr class="tbl-total">
							<th scope="row">what those 12,283 files cost</th>
							<td class="cell-cost" data-label="git worktree add">1358.1 MiB, then npm i and a cold build</td>
							<td class="cell-free" data-label="lane new">0 B, 4.0s</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>

				<p class="lede">
					Edit a file and only that file's blocks un-share. One source edit plus its incremental rebuild rewrote 550 files — <b>106.5 MiB</b>. The other <b>1251.6 MiB</b> stayed shared. The 106.5 MiB goes away when the lane does.
				</p>

				<p class="note">
					Reflink works on APFS, btrfs, XFS with <b>reflink=1</b>, bcachefs, and recent ZFS. Everywhere else, <b>lane init</b> says so up front and <b>lane new</b> leaves a plain worktree — byte-copying a build cache is the expensive thing lane exists to avoid. Opt a path out with <b>git config --add lane.exclude target</b>.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="sec">
			<h2 class="sec-head">Memory</h2>
			<div class="sec-body reveal">
				<p class="tagline tagline-solo">The reason you did it that way, kept next to the code it constrains.</p>
				<p class="lede">
					A note records what must stay true, not what you changed.
				</p>

				<pre class="block"><span class="p">$ </span><b>lane note add src/auth.rs -a "fn verify" \</b>
<b>    "must stay constant-time; early return leaks token length"</b>
noted -&gt; src/auth.rs#fn verify

<span class="p">$ </span><b>lane why src/auth.rs</b>

[fn verify]
  - 01M0B9MBYB · 2026-08-14
    must stay constant-time; early return leaks token length</pre>

				<p class="lede">
					Or leave it in the commit you were already writing. <b>lane install hooks</b> reads the <b>Why:</b> trailer and nothing else — no diff, no commit body, no history import:
				</p>

				<pre class="block"><span class="p">$ </span><b>git commit -am "make verify constant-time"</b>
<i>  ...</i>
<b>  Why: src/auth.rs#fn verify | early return leaks token length</b></pre>

				<p class="note">
					The subject says what you did. The trailer says what must stay true.
				</p>

				<h3 class="eyebrow">Anchors — what the note is about</h3>
				<table class="tbl tbl-pair">
					<tbody>
						{anchors.map(([a, m]) => (
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">{a}</th>
								<td>{m}</td>
							</tr>
						))}
					</tbody>
				</table>
				<p class="note">
					Tree-sitter resolves these, not a regular expression. A span ends where the declaration ends. A <b>#</b> inside a code fence is not a heading.
				</p>

				<h3 class="eyebrow">Freshness — what happens as the code moves</h3>
				<table class="tbl tbl-tier">
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th class="tbl-mark" scope="col"><span class="sr-only">mark</span></th>
							<th scope="col">tier</th>
							<th scope="col">meaning</th>
							<th scope="col">what lane does</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						{tiers.map(([mark, tier, meaning, action, tone]) => (
							<tr data-tone={tone}>
								<td class="tbl-mark">{mark === ' ' ? '·' : mark}</td>
								<th scope="row">{tier}</th>
								<td>{meaning}</td>
								<td>{action}</td>
							</tr>
						))}
					</tbody>
				</table>
				<p class="note">
					Each anchor is hashed on its own span, normalized, so a formatter stales nothing. Editing <b>#script</b> leaves a note on <b>#style</b> alone. A renamed file is followed — <b>lane audit</b> reads git's own rename detection. A drifted note stays flagged until you confirm, replace, or retire it.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="sec">
			<h2 class="sec-head">Agents</h2>
			<div class="sec-body reveal">
				<p class="tagline tagline-solo">The next agent reads the constraints before it edits.</p>
				<p class="lede">
					Two agents can note the same file, and the same anchor, at the same time. Each note is a new file, so nothing locks. Land them in any order; both notes are there afterwards.
				</p>
				<pre class="block"><span class="p">$ </span><b>lane why src/auth.rs</b>

[fn verify]
  - 01M0B9MBYB · 2026-08-14
    must stay constant-time; early return leaks token length
  - 01M0B9MZQ4 · 2026-08-14
    the token bytes must never reach a log, even at debug</pre>
				<p class="note">
					<b>lane init</b> writes a four-line protocol into <b>AGENTS.md</b>. Notes are plain markdown at predictable paths, so an agent finds them with no plugin.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="sec" id="installation">
			<h2 class="sec-head">Installation</h2>
			<div class="sec-body reveal">
				<h3 class="eyebrow">Get the binary</h3>
				<div class="steps">
					<div class="step">
						<code class="step-cmd"><span class="p">$ </span>curl -fsSL https://lane.lukeed.com | sh</code>
						<button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="curl -fsSL https://lane.lukeed.com | sh">copy</button>
						<span class="step-why">
							macOS and Linux, arm64 and x86_64. Lands in <b>~/.local/bin</b>. Set <b>LANE_INSTALL</b> to put it elsewhere, <b>LANE_VERSION</b> to pin a release. Read it first at <a href="/install.sh">lane.lukeed.com/install.sh</a>.
						</span>
					</div>
					<div class="step">
						<code class="step-cmd"><span class="p">$ </span>cargo binstall --git https://github.com/lukeed/lane lane</code>
						<button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="cargo binstall --git https://github.com/lukeed/lane lane">copy</button>
						<span class="step-why">The same prebuilt binary, if you already run binstall.</span>
					</div>
					<div class="step">
						<code class="step-cmd"><span class="p">$ </span>cargo install --git https://github.com/lukeed/lane</code>
						<button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="cargo install --git https://github.com/lukeed/lane">copy</button>
						<span class="step-why">Builds from source. Rust 1.85 or newer, edition 2024.</span>
					</div>
				</div>

				<h3 class="eyebrow">Once per machine</h3>
				<div class="steps">
					<div class="step">
						<code class="step-cmd"><span class="p">$ </span>eval "$(lane shellenv)"</code>
						<button class="copy" type="button" data-copy={'eval "$(lane shellenv)"'}>copy</button>
						<span class="step-why">
							Put it in .zshrc or .bashrc. Without it, <b>lane new</b> prints a path instead of moving you into the lane.
						</span>
					</div>
				</div>

				<h3 class="eyebrow">Once per repository</h3>
				<div class="steps">
					<div class="step">
						<code class="step-cmd"><span class="p">$ </span>cd yourproject &amp;&amp; lane init</code>
						<button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="lane init">copy</button>
						<span class="step-why">
							Scaffolds <b>.lane/</b>, adds the union merge rule and the agent protocol, and reports whether this filesystem does reflink. Commit what it writes.
						</span>
					</div>
					<div class="step">
						<code class="step-cmd"><span class="p">$ </span>lane install hooks</code>
						<button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="lane install hooks">copy</button>
						<span class="step-why">
							Captures <b>Why:</b> trailers from commit messages. Worktrees share a hooks directory, so this covers every lane. Optional.
						</span>
					</div>
					<div class="step">
						<code class="step-cmd"><span class="p">$ </span>lane install skill</code>
						<button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="lane install skill">copy</button>
						<span class="step-why">
							Writes the fuller agent workflow to <b>.agents/skills/lane/SKILL.md</b>. Optional; <b>lane init</b> already leaves a short protocol in AGENTS.md.
						</span>
					</div>
				</div>

				<pre class="block"><span class="p">$ </span><b>cargo run -p lane-tour -- start</b>
<i>builds a throwaway repo next to this checkout and prints its path</i></pre>
				<p class="note">
					The tour runs the real workflows — a note drifting, three lanes landing out of order, two landings colliding — and prints every command before it runs.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="sec foot">
			<h2 class="sec-head">See also</h2>
			<div class="sec-body">
				<ul class="seealso">
					{seealso.map(([href, name, what]) => (
						<li class="seealso-row">
							<a href={href}>{name}</a>
							<span class="seealso-what">{what}</span>
						</li>
					))}
				</ul>
			</div>
		</section>
	</main>

</Page>
